 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "Dunnet Head, not John o' Groats, is the most northerly point of mainland Britain, by a couple of miles. The lighthouse was built in 1831 by Robert Stevenson, grandfather of the author of Treasure Island. On a clear day you can see Orkney and the Old Man of Hoy.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_dunnet_head",
   "title": "Dunnet Head"
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "Dunnet Bay: two miles of sand, the dunes behind it, and the Atlantic in front. The surfers have it to themselves most days. So do the oystercatchers."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "The Castle of Mey, once Barrogill Castle, was a ruin when Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother bought it in 1952. She restored it, gave it back its old name and came every summer and autumn for nearly fifty years. The high walls of the garden keep out the salt wind.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_castle_of_mey",
   "title": "The Castle of Mey"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "The Merry Men of Mey: a tide race off St John's Point where the Pentland Firth's tides meet the Atlantic swell in rows of breaking waves. Nothing about them is merry if you are in a small boat.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_merry_men",
   "title": "The Merry Men of Mey"
  },
  {
   "x": 65,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "John o' Groats is named after Jan de Groot, a Dutchman who ran the ferry to Orkney around 1500. The story goes that he charged a groat, a fourpenny coin, for the crossing. The famous signpost says it is 874 miles to Land's End.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_jan_de_groot",
   "title": "John o' Groats"
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "End to End: Land's End to John o' Groats, the length of Britain, 874 miles by the signpost and rather more by road. People have done it on foot, by bike, on tractors, on unicycles, and once or twice pushing a bath.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_end_to_end",
   "title": "End to End"
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "The Pentland Firth has some of the fastest tidal streams around Britain, racing between the Atlantic and the North Sea. The island out there is Stroma. Its last islanders left in 1962, and now the sheep and the seabirds run it.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_pentland_firth",
   "title": "The Pentland Firth"
  },
  {
   "x": 72,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "The Stacks of Duncansby: great pointed towers of sandstone standing off the cliffs, cut free by the sea. In early summer the cliffs are loud with puffins, guillemots, razorbills and fulmars, and they all have opinions.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_duncansby",
   "title": "Duncansby Stacks"
  },
  {
   "x": 75,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "Duncansby Head lighthouse, built in 1924, marks the north-east corner of mainland Scotland. Further along the cliffs the sea has cut deep clefts called geos, which is Old Norse for exactly that."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "Groatie buckies are the little cowrie shells that wash up on the beaches round here. Find one and you are lucky. Find two and you are lucky, and you have a pocket full of shells.",
   "plaque": "farnorth_groatie_buckies",
   "title": "Groatie Buckies"
  },
  {
   "x": 1,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THURSO 20 miles west. DUNNET HEAD north. The Castle of Mey and John o' Groats east."
  }
 ],
